# Target C-II — the live O--O backbone multishear is a sharp diameter cusp, not a descent route

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Status:** proof-only structural no-go theorem
**Scope:** any finite selected O--O train family whose simultaneous shear diameter-derivative matrix has full column rank; in particular the live `0.017104D-o(D)` backbone on the current `t<=1.844` full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** exact finite arbitrary-O--O multishear; positive relative-interior actual-diameter KKT stress; live-backbone full-column-rank theorem
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The exact finite O--O multishear and the new live-backbone interval-rank theorem fit
together in the opposite way from a descent argument.  On the live backbone, the
current minimizer is a **sharp cusp minimum of diameter inside the entire exact
multishear family**.

Let `tau_1,...,tau_m` be selected O--O trains and let

\[
p_v(\theta)
=p_v+
\sum_{i:v\in S_i}(R_{\theta_i}u_i-u_i)
\tag{SC.1}
\]

be the exact contact-preserving multishear.  For each actual active diameter pair `h`,
orient the pair once and put

\[
\varepsilon_{hi}
=1_{S_i}(y_h)-1_{S_i}(x_h)
\in\{-1,0,1\}.
\]

Define the diameter first-variation matrix

\[
\boxed{
M_{hi}:=\varepsilon_{hi}(r_h\cdot Ju_i),
\qquad r_h=p_{y_h}-p_{x_h}.
}
\tag{SC.2}
\]

Assume `rank M=m`.  If `beta_h>0` is one relative-interior normalized diameter KKT
stress, the cut-resultant identity gives

\[
\boxed{
\beta^T M=0.
}
\tag{SC.3}
\]

Full column rank and strict positivity of `beta` force every nonzero shear direction to
lengthen at least one active diameter edge to first order and shorten at least one
other.  Because the unit sphere is compact, the lengthening is uniformly linear in the
parameter norm for that finite source.  Consequently there are constants
`gamma>0,rho>0` such that

\[
\boxed{
0<\|\theta\|<\rho
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\operatorname{diam}A(\theta)^2
\ge D^2+\gamma\|\theta\|>D^2.
}
\tag{SC.4}

Thus no nontrivial sufficiently small exact O--O multishear can preserve or reduce the
diameter.  In particular, a negative KKT-weighted quadratic curvature of the multishear
cannot become a source-level diameter descent on the live full-rank backbone: the
first-order max-function cusp dominates it.

This is a no-go theorem for one proposed closure mechanism, not a no-go theorem for
C-II.  Closure must leave the pure O--O shear coordinate family, for example by
simultaneously changing additional non-O--O contact-flex coordinates, activating weak
near-contacts, or proving that the assumed full-rank cusp structure is incompatible
with another independent geometric constraint.

---

## 1. KKT stationarity annihilates every shear column

For selected cut `i`, the exact KKT cut resultant is

\[
A_i u_i
=\sum_h\beta_h\varepsilon_{hi}r_h,
\qquad A_i>0.
\tag{SC.5}
\]

Dot with `Ju_i`:

\[
0=A_i u_i\cdot Ju_i
=\sum_h\beta_h\varepsilon_{hi}(r_h\cdot Ju_i)
=\sum_h\beta_h M_{hi}.
\tag{SC.6}
\]

This holds for every selected column, proving (SC.3).

For any coefficient vector `c`, therefore,

\[
\sum_h\beta_h(Mc)_h=0.
\tag{SC.7}
\]

---

## 2. Every nonzero tangent has both diameter signs

Take `c\ne0`.  Full column rank gives

\[
Mc\ne0.
\]

If all entries of `Mc` were nonpositive, then strict positivity of every `beta_h` and
(SC.7) would force every entry to be zero, contradiction.  The same argument applied
to `-c` excludes all entries being nonnegative.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
\max_h(Mc)_h>0,
\qquad
\min_h(Mc)_h<0
\quad(c\ne0).
}
\tag{SC.8}

This is the simultaneous version of Section 157 two-sided bracing.  It is stronger than
saying that each coordinate separately has two signs: no nonzero linear combination of
the selected shears escapes the cusp.

---

## 3. Compactness upgrades sign change to a sharp linear cusp

Use the Euclidean norm on `R^m` and define

\[
g(c):=\max_h(Mc)_h.
\tag{SC.9}
\]

The function `g` is continuous.  By (SC.8), it is strictly positive on the unit sphere.
Since the unit sphere is compact,

\[
\boxed{
\gamma_0
:=\min_{\|c\|=1}g(c)>0.
}
\tag{SC.10}
\]

Positive homogeneity gives

\[
\boxed{
\max_h(Mc)_h\ge\gamma_0\|c\|
\quad\text{for all }c.
}
\tag{SC.11}

The constant is source-specific and may tend to zero along an asymptotic sequence.  No
uniform lower bound in `D` is claimed or needed for the finite local statement.

---

## 4. Exact finite multishear has the same first-order matrix

For an active diameter row `h`, the exact multishear identity is

\[
r_h(\theta)
=r_h+
\sum_i\varepsilon_{hi}(R_{\theta_i}u_i-u_i).
\tag{SC.12}
\]

Because the source and selected family are finite, uniformly for all active diameter
rows as `theta->0`,

\[
R_{\theta_i}u_i-u_i
=\theta_iJu_i+O(\theta_i^2).
\]

Thus

\[
\begin{aligned}
|r_h(\theta)|^2-D^2
&=2\sum_i\varepsilon_{hi}\theta_i(r_h\cdot Ju_i)
+O(\|\theta\|^2)\\
&=2(M\theta)_h+O(\|\theta\|^2).
\end{aligned}
\tag{SC.13}
\]

There is one finite constant `C` such that every active row obeys

\[
|r_h(\theta)|^2-D^2
\ge2(M\theta)_h-C\|\theta\|^2
\tag{SC.14}
\]

in a neighborhood of the origin.

Choose a row attaining the maximum in (SC.11).  Then

\[
\max_h(|r_h(\theta)|^2-D^2)
\ge2\gamma_0\|\theta\|-C\|\theta\|^2.
\tag{SC.15}
\]

For

\[
0<\|\theta\|<\rho:=\gamma_0/C
\]

(with any smaller positive `rho` if `C=0`),

\[
\max_h|r_h(\theta)|^2>D^2.
\tag{SC.16}
\]

Since these are actual source pairs, the full source diameter is also strictly larger
than `D`.  This proves (SC.4).

Strict noncontact preservation is irrelevant to the no-go conclusion: even where the
multishear remains unit-separated, an old diameter row already exceeds `D`.

---

## 5. No higher-order path can sneak through with a nonzero first jet

The same argument applies to any differentiable parameter path

\[
\theta(t)=t^k c+o(t^k),
\qquad c\ne0,
\qquad k\ge1.
\tag{SC.17}
\]

Substituting into (SC.13) gives

\[
\max_h(|r_h(\theta(t))|^2-D^2)
\ge2\gamma_0|t|^k\|c\|+o(|t|^k)>0
\tag{SC.18}
\]

for sufficiently small nonzero `t` on either side.  Reparametrizing a shear as
`theta=t^2c`, for example, does not bypass the first-order cusp; it only moves the same
linear obstruction to the first nonzero parameter jet.

Therefore the weighted second-curvature matrix from the KKT interval identity is not a
hidden descent certificate inside this full-rank family.  Its sign becomes relevant to
diameter only after one first finds a nonzero shear direction in the kernel of `M`, and
full rank says there is none.

---

## 6. Application to the live `0.017104D` backbone

The newly merged live-backbone theorem selects `m` smallest-leaf O--O trains on one
dual-tree path and proves that their diameter derivative matrix

\[
M_{hi}=\varepsilon_{hi}(r_h\cdot Ju_i)
\]

has

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}M=m.}
\tag{SC.19}

The proof uses the fact that a center train outside the selected leaf family has angular
rate zero, eliminating the global Euclidean-rotation kernel.  The current uniform
`t<=1.844` theorem also gives

\[
m>0.017104D-o(D).
\tag{SC.20}

Hence every finite source far enough along that branch has a linear-dimensional exact
contact-preserving O--O multishear family on which the original source is nevertheless
a strict sharp local diameter minimum.

This explains why adding more exact O--O shear coordinates alone does not solve the
current obstruction: mixed rigidity and the positive diameter stress use additional
diameter braces at enough interval locations to cusp-lock the entire family.

---

## 7. Frontier consequence

The exact multishear remains useful as a coordinate system, but not as a standalone
descent family.  The live obligations become more specific:

1. **enlarge the coordinate space:** couple the O--O shears to bounded-face or weak-contact release coordinates so the augmented diameter derivative map acquires a nontrivial critical kernel;
2. **attack cusp compatibility:** show that the interval-rank pattern needed for full
   column rank cannot coexist with the near-disk geometry, KKT resultant total variation,
   or the phase-collapsed rectangle; or
3. **force activation:** use the sharp cusp to quantify how near-contacts outside the
   positive-contact core must activate under attempted O--O motion, then exploit their
   packing/matching constraints.

What is no longer a productive standalone target is proving merely that
`K-diag(A)` has a negative direction on the same full-rank O--O block.  Without a
first-order kernel, such a direction cannot decrease the source diameter.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only sharp-cusp no-go for the live exact O--O multishear
family.  C-II and Level I remain open.
